Specimens in a project that uses the `Ichthyology` catalog format show the fish
measurement form. Sex, life stage, and weight units work the same way in every
taxon group; see [Measurements](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/).

{% aside type="caution" %}

Ichthyology support is in beta. Check exports against your collection's
requirements before relying on them.

{% /aside %}

## Specimen type

Every fish record starts with `Specimen type`:

- `Individual`: one fish with its own measurements.
- `Lot`: many individuals stored together, such as a jar of fish of the same
  species from one collecting event.

Choose `Individual` when the fish is cataloged and stored on its own. Choose
`Lot` when several fish share one catalog number and container. A record keeps
the values of both types, but only the fields of the selected type are shown
and exported.

## Individual measurements

- `Sex`: select from the configured specimen-sex vocabulary.
- `Life stage`: select from the configured life-stage vocabulary.
- `Standard length (mm)`: the length from the tip of the snout to the end of
  the vertebral column, excluding the caudal fin.
- `Total length (mm)`: the length from the tip of the snout to the tip of the
  longest caudal-fin ray.
- `Weight`: specimen weight in the selected unit.
- `Remarks`: additional information about the specimen.

## Lot measurements

- `Total individuals`: the number of fish in the lot, measured or not.
- `Life stage`: the life stage of the lot.
- `Auto-update min and max`: when on, NAHPU fills the ranges below from the
  individuals measured in the lot, and the range fields become read-only. Turn
  it off to enter the ranges by hand, for example when only the smallest and
  largest fish were measured.
- `Min. standard length (mm)` and `Max. standard length (mm)`.
- `Min. total length (mm)` and `Max. total length (mm)`.
- `Min. weight` and `Max. weight`: weight range in the selected unit.
- `Remarks`: additional information about the lot.

`Lot summary` shows, for standard length, total length, and weight, the mean,
standard deviation, number measured (`n`), and range of the individuals in the
lot. It also shows how many of the `Total individuals` have been measured.

## Individuals in a lot

Use the `Individuals` section of a lot to record fish measured one by one.

{% steps %}

1. Select `Add individual`.
2. Under `Identity`, enter the `Tag ID`, or keep the auto-numbered tag. Select
   `Sex` and `Life stage`.
3. Under `Measurements`, enter `SL (mm)`, `TL (mm)`, and weight in the lot's
   weight unit. Add `Remarks` when needed.
4. Select `Save and add another` to continue with the next fish, or `Save` to
   close the form.

{% /steps %}

Select an individual in the list to edit it. Deleting an individual also
unlinks it from any specimen parts and parasites.

## Tag ID numbering

Individual tag IDs can be numbered automatically. Configure the format in
`Settings` > `Specimens` > `Individual tag ID`, which appears only for the
Ichthyology catalog format, or open the menu on the `Tag ID` field and select
`Settings`.

- `Prefix`: text before the number, such as `FT-`.
- `Next number`: the number given to the next individual. Leading zeros are
  kept, so `0007` is followed by `0008`.
- `Suffix`: text after the number, such as `-A`.
- `Next tag`: a preview of the next tag ID.

An auto-numbered tag is marked `Auto-numbered. Edit to override.` Typing a
different tag keeps your value and does not use up the next number. Select
`New number` in the field menu to restore the next auto-numbered tag.

## Parts and parasites

Fish catalogs show parasite-detection fields and the parasite tab. When a lot
has individuals, specimen parts and parasites show an `Individuals` selector.
Use `Add individual` to link the part or parasite to the fish it came from,
such as a fin clip from one tagged fish. See
[Specimen Parts](/en/usages/specimens/parts/).

## Water conditions

Record water conditions on the collecting event rather than on the specimen.
Collecting-event [environmental data](/en/usages/events/) include water
temperature, pH, dissolved oxygen, dissolved oxygen saturation (%),
conductivity (µS/cm), and flow velocity.

## Export

Specimen exports for an Ichthyology catalog add these measurement columns:
`specimenType`, `individualCount`, `sex`, `lifeStage`, `standardLength`,
`totalLength`, `weight`, `weightUnit`, `minStandardLength`,
`maxStandardLength`, `minTotalLength`, `maxTotalLength`, `minWeight`,
`maxWeight`, and `remark`.

The `individuals` column lists the individuals of a lot, separated by `|`.
Each entry looks like
`ID: FT-0001;SL: 52 mm;TL: 61 mm;Weight: 3.1 g;Sex: Female;Life stage: Adult`.
Specimen part and parasite exports list the IDs of their linked individuals,
separated by commas.

In Darwin Core exports, `individualCount` is the lot's `Total individuals`, or
`1` for an individual fish. See [Export Records](/en/usages/export/export-records/).
